From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 11 Aug 94 14:43:50 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-118] Individualism vs. Groupism

Steve Witham wrote 8/6/94: <In fact (ironically) I like to summarize Chaum's
important "making Big Brother Obsolete" argument as: Credentials, not Identity.
(Or, Authenticity, not Identity.)>

Thanks for making this subtle distinction.  It is easy to confuse garden-variety
credentialism, the debilitating kind that closes off potential sources of truth,
with a simple check of identity if the same term is used.

Steve again: <as a sort of Taoist, I would say it's not so much a "non-anchored
self-identity" that's the problem as the compulsion to anchor that drives people
to identify with groups, places, or ideas (Extropianism or individualism, even).
Why we are so keen to have an identity I'm not sure I've ever seen a good
explanation of.>

There are different kinds of Taoism.  The good kind is an active Taoism that
views the Tao as consistently pointing the "Way" towards the chinks in the armor
of Fate that will allow the individual (me, for example), to live and grow
forever.  Questioning the critical importance of identity, which is the essence
of individuality, seems closer to the bad kind of Taoism.  This kind Iris
Murdoch in "Metaphysics as a Guide to Morals" describes as:

  "...A kind of instinctual debased Taoism, arising in a period of exceptional
  scientific and technological progress and popular scientific knowledge, a
  relaxed acceptance as ultimate of a deep impersonal world-rhythm which
  overcomes the awkward dichotomies between good and evil and one individual
  and another.  A sort of neo-Taoism is also part of a popular metaphysic of
  our time.  What is, and not implausibly, envisaged here is an apocalyptic
  change in human consciousness, involving vast social changes and the
  disappearance of old local ideas of individuals and virtues.  A loss of
  sovereignty."

I stand unalterably opposed to a loss of individual sovereignty.  The idea is
morally repugnant because it is death-worship, self-immolation.

grigsby wrote 8/9/94: <Relativism is the opposite of dogmatism...>  etc. and
more stuff about definitions and certainty.

Relativism is Skepticism; rationality tells us we can't be certain of anything,
even that.  This is so because rational people don't like to look too closely at
the limited nature of rationality.  I am 100% absolutely certain of what I am
thinking and what my purposes are, this is my subjective reality.  I am certain
of everything I know, a combination of the facts and the nature of the
epistemological limits that the facts bump up against.  My choice of goals is
not made rationally because the ability to choose goals is below the foundation
of rationality.  There is objective reality, there is only one truth, here and
now, this is what natural science investigates.  The only reason science exists
is because we aren't sure our perceptions of nature are accurate.  We have more
confidence in our perceptions through science because we pat each other on the
back and talk a good story to each other.  Relativism is a free-floating,
unanchored drifting bunch of blithering concepts.  It is dynamically unstable,
it always falls into the attractors of either stagnant dogma in search of
certainty (e.g., Stalinism or Branch Davidianism) or death-worshipping nihilism
(e.g., Buddhism or the French Disease of compost-modernism).  It has nowhere
else to go but to these two losers.  Without absolute certainties, entropy wins
every time.

I would add this from Ayn Rand in "Lexicon":

  'Nobody can be certain of anything' is a rationalization for a feeling of
  envy and hatred toward those who are certain.  'It may be true for you, but
  it's not true for me' is a rationalization for one's inability and
  unwillingness to prove the validity of one's contentions.  'Nobody is
  perfect in this world' is a rationalization for the desire to continue
  indulging in one's imperfections, i.e., the desire to escape morality.
  'It may have been true yesterday, but it's not true today' is a
  rationalization for the desire to get away with contradictions.  Evil
  philosophies are systems of rationalization."

Sasha wrote 8/10/94: <Then, what about different parts of my Society
of Mind - my own personality architecture?  Must some of them get disciplined
for the good of the Whole?>

No, you can throw yourself in front of a bus if you want to.  You can live your
life incoherently if you choose to.  Or, you can pick a purpose in life and make
all your values cohere around it to get something done.  Your choice.  Free
will.  Individualism.

Sasha again: <Why do we view ideal societies as giving complete freedom to their
members, and ideal 'living beings' as commanding complete control over
their parts?>

I don't view ideal society this way.  Complete freedom is impossible because
objective reality, or Fate, doesn't respect the idea.  This is why we band
together in limited freedom to jointly confront and push back Fate.  The parts
of the individual are as Fate to the individual.  The political question is what
limits do we trade off in exchange for help with Fate.

Sasha again: <Do you let a social structure or your own organ perish only
because it's no longer useful?  What if they do it to you?>

"Aye, there's the rub.  To be or not to be." - Shakespeare ("Hamlet")

Sasha again: <If we want to approach these issues intelligently, we better build
a systemic theory of ethics...  after uploading, the system ethics questions
will stare right into our [collective? ;-) ]  face.>

Ack!  A collectivist!  Gag!  Choke!  <g>

I like Miguel Unamuno's ethics, from "The Tragic Sense of Life":

    "For the most fruitful ethic is the ethic of mutual imposition.  My
  endeavor to impose myself upon another, to be and live in him and by him, to
  make him mine, which is the same as making my self his...  He who loves his
  neighbor burns his heart, and the heart, like green wood, groans when it
  burns, and distills itself in tears.  There is no point in taking opium; it
  is better to put salt and vinegar in the soul's wound, for if you fall
  asleep and no longer feel the pain, then you no longer exist.  And the point
  is to exist.  No! to the creed of 'Neither God nor Master!'  Yes! to the
  creed of 'Everyone a God!  Everyone a Master!':  all men striving to become
  gods, all striving to become immortal, and doing so by mastering all others.
    Only the weak resign themselves to final death and substitute some lesser
  passion for the longing to survive in personal immortality.  Among the
  strong, the urge toward self-perpetuation overrides the doubtfulness of
  achieving it, and their overabundance of life overflows the boundaries of
  death.  And it was well said that sloth is the mother of all vice.  Nor
  should we forget that the supreme sloth consists in failing to long madly
  for immortality."

Unamuno thought Nietzsche was a weakling for accepting death in the form of the
"eternal recurrence" nonsense.  I agree with him.  No more namby-pamby
self-decapitating cosmologies.  The individual self is all there is, and it
needs other individual selves to interact with to provide meaning to life.
We're all in this together imposing on each other with every exhalation.

