From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Aug 94 13:39:42 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-122] EPIST: certainty again

>Roger Trigg.  REASON AND COMMITMENT.  Cambridge University Press,
>	1973.
>Dudley Shapere.  REASON AND THE SEARCH FOR KNOWLEDGE.  Dordrecht:
>	Reidel, 1983.
>James Robert Brown: SMOKE AND MIRRORS: HOW SCIENCE REFLECTS
>	REALITY.  London: Routledge, 1994.

Thanks for the references. I haven't run across these books before,
and I look forward to reading them. Which of the three do you think
would serve as the best introduction to the set?

When I think of "a stance that's diametrically opposed to Bartley's" I
think of David Kelley, at least when I'm thinking of the defenders of
absolute certainty. If absolute certainty has a stronger champion than
Kelley, I'd be happy to find one, because THE EVIDENCE OF THE SENSES
(which I admit is all I've read of Kelley's work) is weak, to say the
best. In another important way, people such as Rorty (whose work is
also of little worth) are "diametrically opposed" to evolutionary
epistemology, but the views you've been defending don't seem very
similar to Rorty's.

Here is a mathematical metaphor that I hope conveys my take on this
question. I agree with you that 100% certainty is possible, but only
when I keep in mind that if you can get only (for example) three
significant figures, 100% is the same number as (for example)
99.96%. The same number, when you're talking about the real world,
sensations, perceptions, measurements, in short, the domain of
empirical science. I don't think 100.0% certainty is possible if one
insists on an infinite number of significant zeros after that decimal
point. I shy away from the word "absolute" when discussing certainty,
because "absolute certainty" conveys that idea of an infinite number
of significant figures in a measurement, which idea seems nonsensical
to me for a variety of reasons (measuring technology, including our
senses and nervous systems, and quantum mechanics are two that come to
mind). Just a metaphor, though, and an overextended one at that.

So on the one hand, a measurement (a perception, if you like) with an
infinite number of significant figures is impossible. On the other
hand, 100% and 99.9999999999487% really are the same number for all
empirical purposes. The difference between absolute certainty and very
high degrees of "relative" certainty is insignificant, just like the
difference between the opposite sides of many another vexing semantic
issue in philosophy. In general, I am less annoyed by the claim that
absolute certainty is possible than I am by the claim that the
relativity of certainty dooms us to permanent uncertainty. It does no
such thing.

