Subject: [#94-8-123] EPIST: ah yes, once more!

It's been ten years since I really studied philosophy of science (M.A.
U.Chicago '84), but I did learn a few things.  In particular, I
learned that academic philosophers tend to develop a distinct set of
cognitive skills in the process of becoming professionals - they learn
to be careful in their use of words, methodical in constructing and
deconstructing arguments, and cautious about making claims.

This skill set is *not* optimal for being an engineer or journalist or
parent, but it is pretty good for dealing the questions such
philosophers tend to deal with, namely "foundational" questions that
other fields of study have punted on, questions which tend to hang on
just what one wants to mean by certain common words.

Amateurs who want to discuss a philosophical question, especially on
the net where folks are tempted to post as fast as they can type, are
well advised to try to emulate the cognitive style of professional
philosophers.  Please, think a lot, type a little, and try to be as
precise as possible when you make claims.  Explain what you mean by
common but contentious terms like "rational" or "moral", rather than
assuming we know what you mean.

There are many great philosophical questions worthy of discussion, but
unfortunately most net discussions of them are not worth the bother.
If you want this discussion to be different, please, somebody try to
make a specific precise claim we can argue about.

Robin Hanson, curmudgeon first class

P.S. I'm not saying anyone was sloppy yet, just trying to avoid
future troubles.

